---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import FindMore from '../components/FindMore.astro';
import { directoryStats, href } from '../lib/data';
import { breadcrumbNode, faqNode, pageGraph, webPageNode } from '../lib/schema';

const title = 'How to choose RV storage and what it costs per month';
const description =
  'How to compare covered, enclosed, and outdoor RV storage, what length and height to measure, and how to read a monthly price when a facility publishes one.';
const path = '/guide/';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { label: 'Cost guide', path },
];
const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How much does RV storage cost per month?',
    a: 'SelfStorage.com, read on November 18, 2026, lists national average monthly ranges of $75 to $150 for outdoor storage, $125 to $200 for covered storage, and $150 to $400 for indoor storage. Those ranges are not a quote for a facility in this directory. City pages repeat a price only when that facility publishes one, and this site does not average those prices.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between covered and enclosed RV storage?',
    a: 'Covered storage adds a roof or canopy and leaves the sides open. Enclosed storage is a garage bay or a shared indoor building. Open storage is a marked outdoor space with no roof. A listing is tagged only when the facility page uses that kind of description.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What length and height should I measure?',
    a: 'Measure the stored length, including the hitch or spare tire if it sticks out, and the height at the tallest roof equipment. SelfStorage.com describes typical Class B lengths as 16 to 24 feet, Class C as 20 to 33 feet, and Class A as 28 to 41 feet. Your coach may fall outside those bands, so the measurement on the vehicle wins. Ask whether the lot adds a buffer. A&H Storage in Orlando states that covered parking adds a 5-foot safety buffer to the measured length.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need a power hookup to store an RV?',
    a: 'Many owners use a trickle charger or a battery maintainer, which needs an outlet. A storage outlet is often not a campground pedestal. When a page states 30-amp or 50-amp service, the listing note says so. When the page only says power, do not assume the air conditioner can run.',
  },
];
const schema = pageGraph([
  webPageNode({ title, description, path, dateModified: directoryStats().latest }),
  breadcrumbNode(crumbs),
  {
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: title,
    description,
    dateModified: directoryStats().latest,
    mainEntityOfPage: 'https://sunlightchurch.github.io/rv-storage-finder/guide/',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'RV Storage Finder' },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'RV Storage Finder' },
  },
  faqNode(faqs),
]);
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path={path} schema={schema}>
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <h1>How to choose RV storage and what it costs</h1>
    <p>
      A useful quote names the space, the length, and the total due. The city pages in this directory
      keep a price only when the facility prints one. This guide is how to read those cards and what to
      ask before you pay.
    </p>

    <h2>Measure the vehicle the way the lot will</h2>
    <p>
      Length usually includes whatever sticks out farthest: the hitch, a spare tire, or a ladder. Height
      is the roof air conditioner or satellite, whichever is taller. Width with the slides in is what has
      to clear the door. Ask whether the facility adds a buffer. A&amp;H Storage in Orlando states that
      covered parking adds a 5-foot safety buffer to the measured length. That is one facility’s rule.
      Get the rule for the lot you are renting.
    </p>
    <p>
      SelfStorage.com describes typical lengths as 16 to 24 feet for a Class B, 20 to 33 feet for a Class
      C, and 28 to 41 feet for a Class A. Use those as a rough map, then measure the vehicle you own.
    </p>

    <h2>Covered, enclosed, and open</h2>
    <p>
      Open storage is a marked space on a lot. It is usually the lowest published rate and it leaves the
      finish in the sun, hail, and sap. Covered storage adds a roof or canopy and still has open sides.
      Enclosed storage is a garage bay or a warehouse. It costs more, and it is the option people choose
      for a long sit or a boat that should stay out of the weather.
    </p>
    <p>
      In Tampa and Orlando, ask what the lot does when a hurricane watch is posted. In Phoenix, a roof is
      mostly about the sun. In Houston and Dallas-Fort Worth, ask about hail. Those are questions for the
      facility. A listing here does not claim a storm policy unless that facility’s own page says so.
    </p>

    <h2>Power, dump, and wash</h2>
    <p>
      Power on a storage lot is often a trickle-charge outlet. When a page says 30-amp or 50-amp, the
      listing says that. A wash bay and a dump station are separate facts. Some companies mention a dump
      station at a different address. Advantage RV &amp; Boat Storage in Spring, Texas, describes a dump
      station at the corporate office nearby, so the Spring card does not mark a dump station on that lot.
    </p>

    <h2>Access and security</h2>
    <p>
      A gate code and round-the-clock access are different. Many lots in this directory publish a window
      such as 6 a.m. to 10 p.m. West Tampa Storage lists normal gate hours of 6:00 a.m. to 8:45 p.m. and
      a 24-hour access card for $10 a month. The card says Yes for 24-hour access, and the note carries
      the fee and the ordinary hours. Read both.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cameras and a gate reduce casual theft. They do not replace your own insurance. Ask what the
      facility’s policy covers while the vehicle is stored, and what yours covers.
    </p>

    <h2>What a month of storage costs</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>National monthly ranges published by SelfStorage.com</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Type they name</th>
            <th scope="col">Average monthly cost they list</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Outdoor</th>
            <td>$75 to $150</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Covered</th>
            <td>$125 to $200</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Indoor</th>
            <td>$150 to $400</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="fine">
      Source:
      <a href="https://www.selfstorage.com/storage-types/rv-self-storage" rel="noopener noreferrer">SelfStorage.com RV storage guide</a>,
      read November 18, 2026. These are that site’s national comparison ranges. A rural lot can be lower
      and a coastal enclosed building can be higher. They are not a bid for Tampa, Orlando, Phoenix,
      Houston, or Dallas-Fort Worth.
    </p>
    <p>
      On a city page, a price note keeps the unit size and both numbers when a facility prints a
      promotional rate and a higher in-store rate. CubeSmart in Lutz has published both for the same
      outdoor lengths. This directory does not average them and does not invent a price for a page that
      says to call.
    </p>
    <p>
      The shape of the quote changes by lot. Top Notch RV Storage in Clermont prices open space at $4.50
      per linear foot plus tax and covered space at $7.50 per linear foot plus tax, with a 20-foot
      minimum. West Tampa Storage prints flat monthly tiers by length, from $125 to $170 on the rates
      page we checked. Tax, a gate card, and an admin fee may be extra in either case.
    </p>

    <h2>Questions to ask before you pay</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>What length and height do you measure, and do you add a buffer?</li>
      <li>Is the space covered, enclosed, or open, and what is the door or canopy height?</li>
      <li>Is power included, what amperage is it, and what can it run?</li>
      <li>What are the gate hours, and is 24-hour access an extra fee?</li>
      <li>What is due at move-in, including tax, deposit, and the first month?</li>
      <li>How much notice does it take to leave?</li>
      <li>Where is the dump or wash area if you advertise one?</li>
      <li>What happens to vehicles when a storm is forecast?</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Look up a city</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href={href('/cities/tampa-fl/')}>Tampa RV and boat storage</a></li>
      <li><a href={href('/cities/orlando-fl/')}>Orlando RV and boat storage</a></li>
      <li><a href={href('/cities/phoenix-az/')}>Phoenix RV and boat storage</a></li>
      <li><a href={href('/cities/houston-tx/')}>Houston RV and boat storage</a></li>
      <li><a href={href('/cities/dallas-fort-worth-tx/')}>Dallas-Fort Worth RV and boat storage</a></li>
      <li><a href={href('/storage/')}>Browse by covered, power, dump, and published price</a></li>
    </ul>

    <section class="faq">
      <h2>Questions</h2>
      {
        faqs.map((faq) => (
          <>
            <h3>{faq.q}</h3>
            <p>{faq.a}</p>
          </>
        ))
      }
    </section>
    <FindMore />
  </article>
</Base>
